# What a machine costs an MCU image, what the program pays once, in bytes, and
# how far each may drift.
#
# Read by `scripts/gates/nostd-mcu.sh`. Every figure here is MEASURED, on
# `thumbv7em-none-eabihf` with the toolchain named in MEASURED_WITH, at the
# consumer's `opt-level = "s"` (the probe's Cargo.toml says why), from the
# staticlib `backends/rust/probes/nostd-footprint` produces: linked with one
# machine (`ParallelHistoryProbePolicy`, a `<parallel>` with two `<history>`)
# and with a second, different one (the queue-size probe's flat machine, the
# probe's `second-machine` feature).
#
# WHY A BUDGET AND NOT A TREND. A downstream bare-metal consumer measured the
# engine growing 60-198 bytes per instantiation across two SCE pins and filed
# it (2026-08-21) because nothing on our side could see it: the MCU gate
# compiled but linked nothing, so no gate in this repository had ever weighed a
# section. A budget is what turns "the engine got bigger" from a consumer's bug
# report into our own red.
#
# RE-PINNING IS A DECISION, NOT A CHORE. When a change legitimately moves a
# figure, move it and say in the commit message what the bytes bought. The gate
# fails on a DROP as well, which is not pedantry: the first shape of this probe
# emitted LLVM bitcode instead of machine code and `nm` reported zero symbols,
# so a one-sided gate would have passed forever on an empty measurement. A
# number that falls without a reason means the instrument stopped seeing, and
# that is the failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# WHAT IS WEIGHED, AND WHY NOT THE MACHINE'S SYMBOLS. Until 2026-10-30 this file
# summed the `.text` of every symbol whose name carried the probe machine's
# crate: the runtime's generic code monomorphised for it and the policy
# generated for it. Appendix D now runs once per program over a document's
# tables (docs/adr/0064): the algorithm's code is the runtime's, compiled once,
# and carries no machine's name, while what a machine brings is moving from code
# to `.rodata` (its tables). That instrument would have read the move as a
# shrink, the mistake it had already recorded once for 2026-09-24 when the
# microstep moved between the template and the runtime. So the gate links two
# different machines and holds three figures:
#
#   1. MACHINE_BYTES — the image with two machines minus the image with one,
#      `.text` plus `.rodata`: what one more machine costs, the number a
#      consumer multiplies by its machine count;
#   2. CORE_BYTES — the `.text` of the shared core, the symbols whose own path
#      is in CORE_SYMBOLS, read off the two-machine image (with one machine LTO
#      inlines the core into its only caller): what the program pays once;
#   3. no core symbol carries a type of MACHINE_TYPES: the core is not
#      instantiated per machine. The compiler guarantees this for the core's
#      non-generic functions; the check is what fails if a generic parameter is
#      ever added back to one of them.
#
# The last figures of the old instrument, for the record: 5590 bytes over 40
# symbols with the report, 5538 without (rustc 1.99.1, 2026-10-22). Their
# history — exitInterpreter (+94), the session id (+88), the enabled set sized
# to the document (+98), the strings a `<send>` stopped building (-134, -84),
# the walks that stopped recursing (+474) — is in this file's log.
#
# ⚠ The patterns rely on the v0 symbol-mangling scheme, which the toolchain
# below emits by default: a v0 name carries its concrete generic arguments, so a
# core function instantiated for a machine names it. Under the legacy scheme it
# does not, and check 3 could not see one. MIN_CORE_SYMBOLS is what makes a
# blind instrument a red rather than a smaller number.

# The toolchain the figures below were measured with. Not enforced: the lane
# installs whatever `stable` is on the day, and a toolchain that inlines
# differently moves these bytes with no change of ours. The gate prints this
# beside `rustc --version` when it fails, so a red that is only the toolchain
# says so, and a re-pin names the toolchain it was taken on.
MEASURED_WITH='rustc 1.99.1 (58cf8f9c3 2026-10-13)'

# The shared core, by the path of its own symbols (an awk extended regular
# expression over the demangled name): Appendix D's procedures
# (`helpers::microstep`), the document's tables (`helpers::tables`), the sets of
# states and the run store (`helpers::state_set`, `helpers::run_store`), the
# main event loop (`engine::Turn`), and since docs/adr/0068 the queues as the
# loop sees them (`helpers::event_queue`: a queue holds numbers, and the view
# the loop drives has no machine type), and since docs/adr/0069 the delayed-send
# scheduler (`engine::PullScheduler`, compiled once for each kind of send id the
# program's machines keep; a machine whose kind of send id no other machine
# shares carries its copy inlined into its own `tick`, which this path does not
# match). A machine's implementation of the core's traits
# names the trait inside its `<.. as ..>` and is not matched.
CORE_SYMBOLS='^<?sce_rust_runtime::(helpers::(microstep|state_set|run_store|tables|event_queue)::|engine::(Turn[>:]|PullScheduler<))'

# The probe machines' crates (an extended regular expression): a core symbol
# that names one was instantiated for that machine.
MACHINE_TYPES='sce_nostd_build_probe::|sce_nostd_queue_size_machine::|sce_nostd_plain_machine::'

# The procedures of Appendix D only a `<history>` or a `<parallel>` reaches, by
# their names in `helpers::microstep`: the functions `HISTORY` and `PARALLEL`
# hold (docs/adr/0066). The core reaches them only through a document's tables,
# so an image links them when one of its documents uses the feature and not
# otherwise; the gate holds both (`linked` with the first machine's `<parallel>`
# and `<history>`, `absent` with `plain-documents`).
FEATURE_PROCEDURES='effective_history_targets descend_into_history history_recorded_from record_histories default_regions remove_conflicting_transitions parallel_in_final_state'
FEATURE_PROCEDURE_PATTERN="sce_rust_runtime::helpers::microstep::(${FEATURE_PROCEDURES// /|})"

# What one more machine costs with the macrostep report: the probe built with
# its `macrostep-diagnostics` feature, which turns on the runtime's feature of
# that name — the build a bare-metal crate gets when it asks for the report.
#
# 1774 (2026-10-30), on rustc 1.99.1: images of 192221 and 193995 bytes. Before
# Appendix D ran over tables the same measurement would have been the second
# machine's whole copy of the algorithm; at 49e40c9a72, without the report, it
# was 2865 (see below).
#
# 1774 -> 1734 (2026-10-30): the probe is built at the consumer's
# `opt-level = "s"` (see the probe's Cargo.toml), images of 193380 and 195114.
#
# 1734 -> 1750 (2026-10-30): the tables carry the feature procedures
# (docs/adr/0066), images of 194038 and 195788.
#
# 1750 -> 1726 (2026-10-30): a row is as small as its numbers
# (docs/adr/0067), images of 193896 and 195622.
#
# 1726 -> 1008 (2026-10-30): the engine takes events as numbers and a machine
# brings only what is its own (docs/adr/0068), images of 194197 and 195205. The
# images are not comparable with the ones above: the probe now drives `tick`
# too (`sce_footprint_tick`), the path through the scheduler `step` never
# reached, which the first machine pays for and the second, with no delayed
# send, does not.
#
# 1008 -> 1690 (2026-10-30): the second machine is driven with `tick` as well,
# as a host with a clock drives each of its machines, so its path through the
# scheduler is weighed; images of 194215 and 195905. The scheduler holds event
# numbers (docs/adr/0069), but these two machines keep different kinds of send
# id (`SendIdString<9>` and `ElidedSendId`), so each still carries its own copy.
#
# 1690 -> 1714 (2026-10-30): an empty queue is its storage type's constant and
# `Engine::new_in_place` is out of line (docs/adr/0070); images of 194117 and
# 195831, each smaller than before (194215, 195905). The constructor was 240
# bytes with a 512-byte frame that built both queues on the stack; it is 124-144
# bytes with no temporary. The figure for one more machine rose because the
# first machine, built at four of the probe's entry points, now carries its
# constructor once, and the second carries its own out of line.
#
# 1714 -> 1692 (2026-10-30): a workspace's buffers are not initialised before a
# buffer writes them (docs/adr/0071), images of 194005 and 195697. `run_turn`
# no longer fills the walk's slots in a loop on a temporary and copies them in.
MACHINE_BYTES=1692

# The same without the report, the bound left in place: the build a bare-metal
# crate that writes `default-features = false` gets (docs/adr/0053).
#
# 1672 (2026-10-30), on rustc 1.99.1: images of 191993 and 193665 bytes. At
# 49e40c9a72, the tree before Appendix D ran over tables, the same two images
# measured 189949 and 192814: 2865 for the second machine. The 1193 bytes the
# second machine no longer costs are its copy of the algorithm; the image of
# one machine grew by 2044, which is the algorithm written for any document
# rather than specialised to one, and the tables. The image is smaller from the
# third machine on.
#
# 1672 -> 1630 (2026-10-30): built at the consumer's `opt-level = "s"`, images
# of 193062 and 194692. At "s" the comparison is less kind than at "z": at
# 49e40c9a72 the two images measure 190673 and 192798, 2125 for the second
# machine, so two machines are 1894 bytes larger than before and the second
# machine saves 495. The consumer's kit measured +2444 at two machines, its
# machines being larger: this figure is what the work after ADR 0064 is held to.
#
# 1630 -> 1648 (2026-10-30): the tables carry the feature procedures
# (docs/adr/0066), images of 193718 and 195366.
#
# 1648 -> 1624 (2026-10-30): a row is as small as its numbers
# (docs/adr/0067), images of 193576 and 195200.
#
# 1624 -> 998 (2026-10-30): events as numbers (docs/adr/0068), images of
# 193991 and 194989, the probe driving `tick` as above.
#
# 998 -> 1632 (2026-10-30): the second machine driven with `tick` too, images
# of 194009 and 195641, as above.
#
# 1632 -> 1660 (2026-10-30): the empty queue a constant and the constructor out
# of line (docs/adr/0070), images of 193913 and 195573, as above.
#
# 1660 -> 1638 (2026-10-30): the workspace's buffers uninitialised
# (docs/adr/0071), images of 193801 and 195439.
MACHINE_BYTES_NO_DIAGNOSTICS=1638

# What the program pays once, with the report: the core's `.text`.
#
# 5356 over 38 symbols (2026-10-30), on rustc 1.99.1.
#
# 5356 -> 6066 over 13 symbols (2026-10-30): at `opt-level = "s"` the core's
# small functions are inlined into the large ones, which is what the consumer's
# image carries (6.5 KB there).
#
# 6066 -> 6696 over 20 symbols (2026-10-30): the procedures only a `<history>`
# or a `<parallel>` reaches are called through the tables (docs/adr/0066), so
# they are functions of their own and are not inlined into their callers. A
# program that uses the features pays this; one that does not pays the far
# smaller CORE_BYTES_PLAIN below.
#
# 6696 -> 6702 over 21 symbols (2026-10-30): the rows' fields are decoded, and
# a row's targets by one out-of-line `DocumentTables::targets` (docs/adr/0067).
#
# 6702 -> 7374 over 23 symbols (2026-10-30): what every machine compiled for
# itself around the main loop (taking an event off a queue, the counts, the end
# of a run, the entry into the initial configuration) is the core's, written
# once (docs/adr/0068), and the queue the loop drives is counted with it
# (`helpers::event_queue` joined CORE_SYMBOLS, 152 of the bytes). The machine's
# figure above fell by 718 while the core gained 672: the core is paid once, a
# machine's figure for each.
#
# 7374 -> 7312 over 23 symbols (2026-10-30): a buffer reads only the prefix it
# wrote, over slots nothing initialised (docs/adr/0071).
CORE_BYTES=7312

# The same without the report.
#
# 5346 over 37 symbols (2026-10-30), on rustc 1.99.1.
#
# 5346 -> 6048 over 13 symbols (2026-10-30), at `opt-level = "s"`.
#
# 6048 -> 6678 over 20 symbols (2026-10-30), the feature procedures called
# through the tables (docs/adr/0066), as above.
#
# 6678 -> 6684 over 21 symbols (2026-10-30), the rows decoded (docs/adr/0067).
#
# 6684 -> 7266 over 23 symbols (2026-10-30), events as numbers and the queue
# counted with the core (docs/adr/0068), as above.
#
# 7266 -> 7204 over 23 symbols (2026-10-30), the workspace's buffers
# uninitialised (docs/adr/0071), as above.
CORE_BYTES_NO_DIAGNOSTICS=7204

# A program none of whose documents has a `<history>` or a `<parallel>`, without
# the report (docs/adr/0066, decision 4): the probe's `plain-documents`, whose
# first machine is `plain_footprint_probe.scxml` and whose second is the
# queue-size probe's. What one more such machine costs, and the core such a
# program pays once — the figure a consumer of flat or nested compound
# machines carries, and the one the features' procedures must stay out of.
#
# 1618 and 4528 over 11 symbols (2026-10-30), on rustc 1.99.1: images of 190566
# and 192184. The core of such a program before docs/adr/0066 was the 6048 of
# the configuration above: 1520 bytes of it were the features'. The same two
# documents at 49e40c9a72, the tree before Appendix D ran over tables, measure
# 188064 and 190137: two of them still cost 2047 bytes more than they did
# there, because each machine's own engine (`Engine<P>` and its hooks) is
# 1.0-1.5 KB where its copy of the algorithm was 1.8-3.8 KB, and the core is
# paid once on top. That is the figure the engine's next change is held to.
#
# 1618 -> 1594 and 4528 -> 4474 over 12 symbols (2026-10-30): a row is as small
# as its numbers (docs/adr/0067), images of 190446 and 192040. The core is
# smaller although it now decodes the rows: what the decoding costs is one
# `DocumentTables::targets` of 104 bytes, and every caller of it lost more.
#
# 1594 -> 1076 and 4474 -> 5058 over 14 symbols (2026-10-30): events as
# numbers (docs/adr/0068), images of 191050 and 192126 with the probe driving
# `tick`. Without `tick`, the same change measured 192040 -> 191476 for the two
# machines: what a machine brought around the loop is the core's now, and the
# entry set names where the current state is read from rather than a second
# pass dereferencing the targets again (`enter_states` 1050 -> 952).
#
# 1076 -> 1456 and 5058 -> 5384 over 16 symbols (2026-10-30): the second machine
# driven with `tick` too, images of 191070 and 192526. Both machines keep the
# zero-size `ElidedSendId`, and the scheduler holds event numbers
# (docs/adr/0069), so they share one scheduler: `PullScheduler<ElidedSendId>`'s
# 326 bytes are the core's. With the scheduler generic over the machine's event,
# as before, the same two images measured 192730: 204 bytes more.
#
# 1456 -> 1496 (2026-10-30): the empty queue a constant and the constructor out
# of line (docs/adr/0070), images of 190966 and 192462, each smaller than before
# (191070, 192526), as above.
#
# 1496 -> 1460 and 5384 -> 5326 over 16 symbols (2026-10-30): the workspace's
# buffers uninitialised (docs/adr/0071), images of 190874 and 192334.
MACHINE_BYTES_PLAIN=1460
CORE_BYTES_PLAIN=5326

# Drift allowed either way before the gate speaks, in BYTES, for each figure.
# Wide enough that an unrelated inlining decision does not cry wolf, tight
# enough that one more copy of a cold path (the 2026-08-21 report's ~170 B per
# instantiation) does. Carried over from the instrument this file held before,
# where it was the 8% of the then 1666-byte figure it started as.
TOLERANCE_BYTES=133

# The instrument's own floor: below this many core symbols the probe is not
# measuring the core at all — a moved module, a bitcode staticlib, a driver
# that stopped driving. 37 and 38 at `opt-level = "z"`; 13 at "s", where the
# small functions are inlined into the large ones; 20 once the feature
# procedures became functions of their own (docs/adr/0066), and 11 in the
# `plain-documents` image, which links none of them. The floor sits under
# those, above the none a blind instrument sees.
MIN_CORE_SYMBOLS=8

# ── The worst-case stack ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY A STACK BUDGET. `.text` is what an image costs; the stack of the deepest call chain is what
# a machine is built under on a bare-metal target, and a change can make a frame larger and
# leave `.text` where it was: the engine's three buffer-holding frames of 520-550 bytes each
# (2026-10-22) moved no figure above. `tools/mcu_stack/worst_case_stack.py` reads the probe's
# disassembly: each function's frame (what its prologue takes, the return address among it) and
# the largest sum along the calls of the object, from the roots below.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. Static, and the probe's own profile (`opt-level = "s"`, fat LTO): inlining
# moves a frame from one function to its caller, so the number is a property of this build, and
# a consumer's image, with its own code and toolchain around the engine, will differ even at the
# same `opt-level`; what it holds is that the engine's chain does not grow
# unseen. It is read off the two-machine image, where the core reaches each machine through a
# trait object and the calls are indirect.
#
# WHAT IS NOT STATIC IS STATED HERE, AND PRINTED BY THE GATE. A call through a trait object is
# followed into every implementation the caller may reach (STACK_DYN, `caller=callees`), the
# way class-hierarchy analysis resolves a virtual call: Appendix D's procedures reach the
# machines' `Behaviour` hooks, the main loop their `MainLoop` and `Behaviour` hooks. A function
# reached through a pointer the host gave the engine (its clock reading, which an inlining may
# have put in any function) is taken to need STACK_INDIRECT_BYTES; the formatting machinery
# calls whatever `Display` it is handed, so `core::fmt::write` is taken to need STACK_ASSUME
# below its own frame. There is no recursion to bound: Appendix D's walks run over an explicit
# stack of steps. An indirect call or a cycle this file does not account for fails the run, so
# none is assumed away.
#
# 1776 (2026-10-22), on rustc 1.99.1, both configurations: `initialize` through the main loop,
# `complete_macrostep`, `take_event`, `microstep` and `enter_entry_set` to a `Vec<u8, 256>`
# clone and `memcpy`. The tolerance is the footprint's, in kind: wide enough for an inlining
# decision, narrow enough that one more buffer in a frame of that chain trips it.
#
# 1776 -> 1584 -> 1616 -> 1056 (2026-10-22): the generated `<send>` stopped building strings,
# the walks of Appendix D stopped recursing, and a delayed send stopped copying an id of the
# string bound; the chain the figure held became the runtime's own, `initialize` (232 bytes,
# the walks' stack and the entry set) through the main loop, `take_event`, `select_transitions`
# (224), `remove_conflicting_transitions`, `compute_exit_set` and `effective_target_states`.
#
# 1056 -> 1232 (2026-10-30): Appendix D runs once per program over tables (docs/adr/0064), and
# the figure is now read off the two-machine image, where the core is not inlined into the
# machine's `Engine`. The chain is `initialize` (240) through the machine's
# `run_main_event_loop` (232, the workspace of bit vectors the whole call lends), the shared
# `Turn::run_main_event_loop` (40), `take_event`, `take_microstep` (272), `enter_states` (296),
# `walk_entry` (104) and a set's search. What the bytes bought: the core's frames are functions
# of their own, called once per program, instead of a copy inlined into every machine.
#
# 1232 -> 1368 (2026-10-30): the probe is built at the consumer's `opt-level = "s"`, which
# inlines the core's small functions into its large ones and their frames with them.
#
# 1368 -> 1392 (2026-10-30): the feature procedures are reached through the tables
# (docs/adr/0066), and the chain is `initialize` (360) through `run_main_event_loop` (328),
# `Turn::run_main_event_loop`, `take_event`, `take_microstep` (232), `enter_states` (224),
# `walk_entry` (136) and `default_regions` (48), now a function of its own.
#
# 1392 -> 1120 (2026-10-30), both configurations: one workspace for a whole turn,
# built in `Engine::run_turn` and nowhere else (docs/adr/0068). The chain is
# `step` (24) through `run_turn` (328, the workspace), `Turn::run` (104),
# `take_event`, `take_microstep` (232), `remove_conflicting_transitions` (160),
# `compute_exit_set`, `effective_target_states_on` and the history's
# `effective_history_targets`.
STACK_ROOTS='Engine<sce_nostd_build_probe::ParallelHistoryProbePolicy>>::step Engine<sce_nostd_build_probe::ParallelHistoryProbePolicy>>::initialize Engine<sce_nostd_build_probe::ParallelHistoryProbePolicy>>::process_event'
STACK_RECURSION=''
STACK_ASSUME='core::fmt::write=256'
STACK_INDIRECT_BYTES=64
# Space-separated `caller=callees` pairs of regular expressions; `\s` rather than a space inside
# a pattern, since the list is split on whitespace.
#
# The core calls through two kinds of pointer: a machine's hooks (`Behaviour`, `MainLoop`), and
# since docs/adr/0066 the feature procedures a document's tables carry. The private fields of
# `HistoryProcedures` and `ParallelProcedures` make `HISTORY` and `PARALLEL` their only values,
# so a call through one reaches FEATURE_PROCEDURES and nothing else. Which field a call reads is
# not in the disassembly, but which calls a function's source makes is: a function's pointer
# calls reach the hooks and the feature procedures its own source calls lead to, and an inlining
# moves code only along those calls. So each core function below names what its calls may
# reach, and no more. Following every pointer call into every hook and procedure would be sound
# too, but it charges impossible paths and makes cycles that do not exist (a `Behaviour` hook
# asks isInFinalState, whose `<parallel>` procedure would then "reach" a procedure that reaches
# the hook again).
#
# STACK_DYN_COMPLETE names the core's own functions: an indirect call in one that no caller
# below matches fails the run, rather than passing as a host callback of STACK_INDIRECT_BYTES,
# and what its callers state is all it reaches. A machine's own functions keep the allowance,
# for the host's pointers (its clock) an inlining may put there; what they may reach of the
# core's pointers is named as well: a `Behaviour` hook asks isInFinalState and nothing else of
# the features, and the machine's `Engine` and `MainLoop` code drives the whole main loop.
P='sce_rust_runtime::helpers::microstep::'
HOOKS='\sas\ssce_rust_runtime::(engine::MainLoop|helpers::microstep::Behaviour)>::'
EFFECTIVE="^${P}effective_history_targets\$"
WALK="^${P}(descend_into_history|history_recorded_from|default_regions)\$"
ALL_FEATURES="^${FEATURE_PROCEDURE_PATTERN}\$"
STACK_DYN="^${P}(effective_target_states_on|compute_exit_set|remove_conflicting_transitions)\$=${EFFECTIVE}"
STACK_DYN+=" ^${P}(walk_entry|listed_target)\$=${WALK}"
STACK_DYN+=" ^${P}(add_transition_to_entry_set|compute_entry_set|current_state|enter_states|enter_entry_set)\$=(${HOOKS}|${EFFECTIVE}|${WALK})"
STACK_DYN+=" ^${P}select_transitions\$=(${HOOKS}|^${P}remove_conflicting_transitions\$|${EFFECTIVE})"
STACK_DYN+=" ^${P}(exit_states|compute_states_to_exit|exit_interpreter|execute_transition_content)\$=(${HOOKS}|^${P}record_histories\$|${EFFECTIVE})"
STACK_DYN+=" ^${P}is_in_final_state\$=^${P}parallel_in_final_state\$"
STACK_DYN+=" ^(${P}microstep\$|<sce_rust_runtime::engine::Turn>)=(${HOOKS}|${ALL_FEATURES})"
STACK_DYN+=" \sas\s${P}Behaviour>::=^${P}parallel_in_final_state\$"
STACK_DYN+=" ^<sce_rust_runtime::engine::Engine<|\sas\ssce_rust_runtime::engine::MainLoop>::=${ALL_FEATURES}"
STACK_DYN_COMPLETE='^<?sce_rust_runtime::(helpers::microstep::|engine::Turn[>:])'
STACK_MIN_FUNCTIONS=200
#
# 1120 -> 1032 (2026-10-30): the workspace's buffers are not filled before a
# turn lends them, so `run_turn` holds them once (328 -> 240) (docs/adr/0071).
STACK_BYTES=1032

# The most a root's OWN frame may take: what an entry point a host calls keeps
# in the host's frame when it is inlined there. Held apart from the worst case,
# which cannot see it: a buffer that moves from a callee into the root stays on
# the same chain.
#
# 64 (2026-10-30): `initialize` takes 16 and `step` 24 in every configuration.
# At ADR 0064 the turn's workspace was built in functions that inline into them,
# and a consumer measured its task setup's frame 4256 bytes larger, its stepping
# function's 1320 larger, until `Engine::run_turn` was kept out of line
# (docs/adr/0068). One more workspace in a root is hundreds of bytes over this.
STACK_ROOT_FRAME_BYTES=64

# The roots and the worst-case stack of the `plain-documents` image, whose first machine is
# `PlainFootprintProbePolicy`.
#
# 1152 (2026-10-30): `initialize` (272) through `run_main_event_loop` (240), the shared main
# loop, `take_microstep` (216), `enter_states` (224) and `walk_entry` (136); no feature
# procedure is linked, so none is on it.
#
# 1152 -> 1128 (2026-10-30): a row is as small as its numbers (docs/adr/0067), and the frames
# of the chain that decoded targets inline are smaller.
#
# 1128 -> 928 (2026-10-30): one workspace per turn in `run_turn` (240), and the
# entry no longer dereferences the targets twice (docs/adr/0068).
STACK_ROOTS_PLAIN='Engine<sce_nostd_plain_machine::PlainFootprintProbePolicy>>::step Engine<sce_nostd_plain_machine::PlainFootprintProbePolicy>>::initialize Engine<sce_nostd_plain_machine::PlainFootprintProbePolicy>>::process_event'
#
# 928 -> 880 (2026-10-30): `run_turn` 240 -> 192, the workspace held once
# (docs/adr/0071).
STACK_BYTES_PLAIN=880
STACK_TOLERANCE_BYTES=256
